<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Alcoraz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1096">1096</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Alcoraz was fought in November 1096 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, where Peter I of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> defeated the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, which had Castilian support. Huesca surrendered shortly afterwards and became a royal seat of the kingdom. A later legend that Saint George fought for the Aragonese gave rise to the Cross of Alcoraz with four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>' heads in the arms of Aragon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcoraz.jpg" alt="Battle of Alcoraz" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeronimo Martínez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Creu-Alcoraz-1524-Jeronimo-Martinez-Retablo-San-Jorge.Salvador-Merce-Teruel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1094 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a>, King of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, laid siege to the strategic Muslim city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, directing it from camps at the Castle of Montearagón and at Pueyo de Sancho, a temporary fortress on San Jorge hill 3 km west of the city. In June 1094 he was struck and killed by an arrow while inspecting the ramparts around Huesca, and his son and heir Peter I took command and kept up the siege for nearly two and a half years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In November 1096 a relief army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mustain-ii">Al-Musta'in II</a>, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> to break the siege. It was supported by Castilian forces under Counts García Ordóñez de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najera">Nájera</a> and Gonzalo Núñez de Lara, who represented King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>. On 15 November 1096 the two armies met on the fields of Alcoraz, between the walls of Huesca and the Castle of Montearagón, and Peter I's army defeated the relief force. Less than two weeks later Huesca surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Later legends held that Saint George appeared above the Aragonese crown during the battle to inspire a Christian army heavily outnumbered. The story parallels that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clavijo">Battle of Clavijo</a>, more than two hundred years earlier, at which Saint James was said to have appeared before the forces of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>. The heraldic shield known as the Cross of Alcoraz, made to commemorate the victory, was later adopted as the personal arms of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a> and then placed in the flag of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>, where it remains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Alcoraz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was killed besieging Huesca, which his son Peter I took after the <b>Battle of Alcoraz</b> in 1096; Barbastro followed in 1100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
